    {
      "block_id": "2605.06070_p6_b22",
      "text": "This result shows that the posterior expected quality gap consists of the prior mean difference\naugmented by a correction term induced by the selection event Qw > Ql. This quantity exhibits the\nfollowing properties, making it suitable as a surrogate for the reward model. When the observed\noutcome is consistent with the prior ordering (i.e., \u00b5w \u226b\u00b5l), the correction term becomes negligible\nand the posterior expectation is dominated by the prior mean difference. In contrast, when the\noutcome is unexpected (i.e., \u00b5w < \u00b5l), the correction term becomes dominant, enforcing a strictly\npositive expected gap consistent with the observed comparison.\n",
      "bbox": [
        107.69,
        197.02,
        504.35,
        272.51
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        430,
        788,
        2017,
        1090
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 646,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.06070_p6_b23",
